Chapter: RocFoldSum
  cites Foreword chapter Console

 Ported from roc-lang/roc, src/eval/test/eval_closure_recursion_tests.zig,
 "inspect: inline fold sum lambda". The Roc source is the whole case:

     (|list, init, step| {
         var $state = init
         for item in list {
             $state = step($state, item)
         }
         $state
     })([1, 2, 3, 4], 0, |acc, x| acc + x)

 and Roc expects 10.

 Two adaptations, both named. Roc's numeric literals default to a decimal
 type, so its expectation reads `10.0` where a Codex Integer prints `10`;
 the arithmetic is the same and only the spelling differs. And Roc's loop
 is an imperative `for` over a `var`, which Codex does not have -- the
 accumulation is a named recursive definition instead, which is Codex's
 only loop.

 That second one costs something and it is worth being precise about what.
 The IMMEDIATE APPLICATION survives: the outer lambda is still a literal in
 function position, still takes `step` as a function-typed parameter, and
 is still applied to three arguments in the same expression that builds it.
 What does not survive is the mutable binding across loop iterations, and
 no port of this file can carry that.

 What it asks that no row in this corpus asks yet: a lambda LITERAL in
 function position, applied immediately, receiving another lambda literal
 as an argument and handing it on to a named definition that calls it.
 `iterate-zip-test.codex` passes lambda literals to `list-zip-with-generic`
 but the receiver is a name, not a literal under application; nothing here
 builds a closure and enters it in one expression. That is the
 neighbourhood of findings 39 and 40 -- a closure's arity, and what happens
 when application and construction meet.

Section: Functions

  fold-loop : List Integer, Integer, (Integer, Integer -> Integer), Integer -> Integer
  fold-loop (xs) (acc) (step) (i) =
   if i >= list-length xs then acc
   else fold-loop xs (step acc (list-at xs i)) step (i + 1)

Section: Entry

  opening : [Console] Nothing
  opening = act
   let total = (\xs base step -> fold-loop xs base step 0) [1, 2, 3, 4] 0 (\acc x -> acc + x)
   in print-line-uni (show total)
  end
